This is an app to help people archive websites using the archive.today service. It first cleans the input URL of any trackers or unnecessary params so the archived page can be more likely to be found and use by another archivist or researcher. I am in progress of adding a new feature, to allow the user to download videos using the yt-dlp service. I do this using Chaquopy and pure python packages only.
 Keep in mind the following:

1.  This project must be flexible to handle lots of different download scenarios, from direct download, to downloading and then transcoding, from supporting DASH downloads with separate streams. All code blocks must be site agnostic and focus on being resilient to the diversity of possible options.
2.  The formats presented to the user are a simplification of the actual formats available, which I refer to as back-end formats. The app needs to be flexible to the video format types available and choose a correct strategy. For example merge with Media3 Transformer if the video is a DASH video-HLS audio separated file.Making sure that if there is an audio only option, only the highest quality audio option is shown to the user. We assume the user will always want the best audio, they wouldn’t waste their time with poor audio.
3.  The user expects videos to have audio. This is important to keep in mind as the back-end formats are often just video-only options as the audio and video streams are separated, which is fine. If this scenario appears, we will download the quality the user-selected video and merge it with the best audio track together, similarly to how the yt-dlp project uses ffmpeg when this situation arrises. 
4. For debugging purposes, using feature gates, the download fallback options are currently disabled. I want to make sure our code for the first download attempt is actually working. 
5. The app will only support 64-bit devices. If the device isn't 64-bit then this download feature won't be an option in the share menu. However, for the devices that don't meet the requirements the rest of the app's features will still be available. 
6. Downloads are made to the user's Download folder. The popup request for download quality will show a simplified selection of available formats from the yt-dlp's list of formats that are available from the site the video is being downloaded from. Don't offer anything less than 360p if a quality available is at least 360p. If at least 720p is available, then don't offer anything less than 720p. And if videos beyond 60fps are available only show the highest and the lowest fps options for each available quality. So if the video is available at {(1080p 30fps, 1080p 60fps, 1080p 120fps), (720p 30fps, 720p 60fps, 720p 120fps)} only show {(1080p 30fps, 1080p 120fps), (720p 30fps, 720p 120fps)}
7. The app needs to keep track of downloads until they are done. This way the app can attempt to resume where it left off if it was interrupted. The app also needs to handle any network interruptions and have retry logic. 
8. We shouldn't need special handling per website - yt-dlp already knows how to handle each platform. Do not overcomplicate this. Trust that yt-dlp has built-in intelligence for each supported site. Rely on dynamic and fluid approaches to adjusting the option configs for yt-dlp to ensure the download and merge is successful. Android can handle merging DASH files just fine. 
9. In the download options we specify to yt-dlp we should always set `ignoreerrors` to be true; as we will handle merging or correcting issues ourselves.
10. We need to set the yt-dlp option no-direct-merge as True, as it is essential for the MediaMuxer-based architecture
11. Don't let yt-dlp handle merging - it will try to use ffmpeg we don't have
12. Don't convert everything - only process files that actually need it
13. Preserve original quality when possible

